WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.296
Preflight checks should verify aircraft condition, batteries, controller, firmware state,

00:00:04.296 --> 00:00:08.912
storage, payload, lighting, home point, navigation status, signal, return settings, weather,

00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:11.010
site control, and crew communications.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.527
Conduct a final darkness-specific hazard review.

00:00:13.527 --> 00:00:15.206
Use a deliberate takeoff.

00:00:15.206 --> 00:00:19.821
Establish stable control and confirm orientation before departing the recovery area.

00:00:19.821 --> 00:00:24.856
Keep the initial route simple and maintain altitude and clearance appropriate to

00:00:24.856 --> 00:00:27.374
the approved plan and site hazards.

00:00:27.374 --> 00:00:31.570
During flight, monitor aircraft lights, visual position, telemetry, battery, signal,

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.088
navigation behavior, weather, people, and obstacles.

00:00:34.088 --> 00:00:40.382
Payload work should pause whenever the pilot needs attention for basic control or situational awareness.

00:00:40.382 --> 00:00:44.578
Return-to-home depends on correct home point, navigation, route, altitude assumptions,

00:00:44.578 --> 00:00:47.095
obstacles, and an available recovery area.

00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:53.809
Review those assumptions before launch and remain prepared for manual intervention according to training and procedures.

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.166
If orientation is lost, avoid rapid speculative inputs.

00:00:57.166 --> 00:01:01.362
Stabilize if possible, communicate, use verified telemetry and visual cues,

00:01:01.362 --> 00:01:04.718
and execute the planned return or landing action.

00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:10.593
If signal degrades, do not continue farther because control has not yet been lost.

00:01:10.593 --> 00:01:14.369
Abort criteria must trigger early enough to preserve options.

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:17.726
Lighting failure, unexpected obstacles, weak signal, navigation uncertainty,

00:01:17.726 --> 00:01:20.244
falling visibility, battery anomaly, crew fatigue,

00:01:20.244 --> 00:01:24.020
or privacy concerns can all justify ending the mission.
